- Class names are the wiki's. Guides from 2025 filed the Anvil under Pistols;
  Embark's 2.0 notes call the Bantam a "hand cannon", and the wiki now files
  both there.

## Rarity: one per gun, so no `tiers`

Every ARC Raiders gun has **one** rarity, which is part of the gun: the
format's `tiers` (D134) are for a game where the same gun comes in several
rarities (Warzone), so a Smart Action for "Epic" would be the same as one
linked to the Epic guns. They are listed here instead, and the owner can link
an action to these guns by name (D134's linking):

| Rarity | Guns |
|---|---|
| Common | Kettle, Rattler, Ferro, Stitcher, Hairpin |
| Uncommon | Arpeggio, Stiletto, Il Toro, Burletta, Bantam |
| Rare | Renegade, Canto, Venator, Anvil (since 2.0), Torrente, Osprey, Rascal |
| Epic | Tempest, Bettina, Bobcat, Vulcano, Hullcracker |
| Legendary | Aphelion, Dolabra, Jupiter, Equalizer |

Checked 2026-10-10 against the wiki's rarity categories (Common, Uncommon,
Rare, Epic and Legendary Weapons: 5, 5, 7, 5, 4 guns, exactly the table) and,
where Embark says it, its notes: Bantam uncommon and Anvil Green to Blue (2.0),
Canto Rare and Dolabra Legendary (1.22.0). Embark's notes call Uncommon
"green" and Rare "blue"; guides give Common grey, Epic purple, Legendary
orange. Whether the in-raid weapon panel shows the colour at all is unknown.

**Levels.** A gun's level is a Roman numeral after its name (Ferro I to IV;
legendaries have none), plus "Amplified" since 2.0. The catalog lists each gun
once; a level is not a different gun. If the HUD prints "FERRO IV", the
reader's `--span words` (the gun is the first words of the line) reads Ferro.

`fireMode` is set where it is one of the format's five; break-action (Ferro,
Rascal), lever-action (Renegade) and slide-action (Hairpin) guns have none.
The wiki calls the Bantam double-action and the Anvil single-action; both are
filed `semi` (one shot per trigger pull); the Aphelion's 2-round burst is
`burst`.
The format's `ammo` words are Apex Legends' and have no "medium", so ammo
(Light, Medium, Heavy, Shotgun, Energy Clip, Launcher) is not in the catalog.

## On the HUD (how each thing shows)

From the wiki's [Game UI page](https://arcraiders.wiki/wiki/Game_UI) and
[Settings page](https://arcraiders.wiki/wiki/Settings), and Embark's 1.22.0
notes; **the weapon panel itself is not described anywhere public**:

- Third-person camera. **Bottom-right: the equipment panel**, which "shows the
  weapon currently in use along with ammunition remaining and the item
  equipped in the last accessed quick use slot" (wiki). Whether the gun's
  **name is text**, its case and font, whether it fades, and whether a rarity
  colour shows there are all **unconfirmed**: no guide, review, wiki page or
  settings page found on 2026-10-10 says so, and none describes a screenshot
  of it.
- Also bottom-right, per Steam players (not Embark): a small session-ID number
  string that cannot be hidden. A name reader must not take it for the gun.
- Bottom-left: shield and health bars (a squad's too). Top-centre: compass and
  round timer. Centre: reticle (a circle for shotguns) and the reload circle
  ("Show Reload Progress", on by default).
- "HUD Position" (Stretched by default), "Font Size" (Small by default, needs
  a restart), and HUD visibility ("Visible", "Crosshair and Interactions Only",
  "Crosshair Only", "Hidden"; F10 on PC, a Gameplay setting on console since
  1.22.0) move or remove things; going by their names, every mode but "Visible"
  hides the panel (not checked).

## HUD states

**From public sources, not checked on a real frame.** What the Core could read
in each state, if the panel turns out to print the name:

| State | What is on screen | What the Core could read |
|---|---|---|
| In raid, on foot | Full HUD: equipment panel bottom-right, bars bottom-left, compass top-centre | The gun's name (or icon) and ammo in the panel; the compass bar as a "topside" sign |
| Aiming (weapon raised) | Reticle appears at centre; the HUD stays (third-person, no full-screen scope found described) | Same as on foot; the reticle shape tells shotgun (circle) from the rest |
| Menu / inventory open | The five-tab menu (Inventory, Crafting, Map, Logbook, plus settings) over the game; the inventory opens on its own when searching a container | Nothing: hold the last gun. Swapping weapons happens here, so re-read once it closes |
| Quick-use / ping / emote wheel | A radial menu at centre | Nothing new; hold the last gun |
| Downed | Not described publicly beyond settings being locked "while downed" | Unknown; treat as no gun |
| Extraction | Extraction timing is on the Map, not the HUD; the lift and the round-end screens are not described | Unknown; treat as no gun |
| Hideout / lobby (Speranza) | Menus between raids (stash, workshop, traders); no in-raid HUD | Nothing; a sign that no raid is running |

## Which reading: name reading, or pass

**Name reading if the panel prints the name**, else **picture teaching** of
the gun's icon; not Rainbow-style, because ARC Raiders has no characters to
pick. Evidence: the wiki says the bottom-right panel shows "the weapon
currently in use" but not how. Confidence that it is text: **low to medium**,
unconfirmed. One real frame settles it, and the catalog carries `review` until
then.

## Update the list

Add a new gun at the end of its class with the update as `from` and Embark's
notes as a source; set `asOf` and `season`; run
`node dashboard/tools/test-game-data.js`.

## Pictures (D474, 2026-10-24)

Embark publishes no weapon catalogue: the only official weapon renders on its own
host are the Frozen Trail bumpers of the Bantam and Stiletto
(`assets.arcraiders.com/media/...AR_Bumper_Weapon_Bantam_16_9.jpg`,
`...Stiletto_16_9.jpg`), 16:9 scenes rather than item pictures. So every picture
in `weapons/` is the game's own **in-game item icon** (Embark's art, the level IV
icon where the gun has levels), as the community wiki arcraiders.wiki serves it
on the gun's page, cropped to the weapon and scaled to fit 480 x 300, transparency
kept. The host is the wiki's, not Embark's: the owner decided on 2026-10-24 to use
the real official weapon art and handle licensing later (D474), and the test's
publisher rule for ARC Raiders accepts arcraiders.wiki for this reason. Delete a
`.png` and set the gun's `image` to `null` and the app draws the class symbol.

